ithin the High Renaissance as a pivotal example of its artistic ideals and innovations."}, {"sourceSlug": "duke-ludovico-sforza", "sourceName": "Duke Ludovico Sforza", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "the-last-supper", "targetName": "The Last Supper", "context": "Duke Ludovico Sforza commissioned The Last Supper as part of a broader initiative to enhance Milan’s cultural prestige."}], "places": [{"name": "Milan, Italy", "role": "Location of the artwork"}, {"name": "Santa Maria delle Grazie, Italy", "role": "Original housing of the mural"}, {"name": "Florence, Italy", "role": "Cultural hub of the Renaissance"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: The Last Supper", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "the-last-supper"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q128910", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Last_Supper_(Leonardo)"], "tags": ["Italy", "work of art", "Europe", "Medical Institutions & Hospitals", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "The Last Supper left a notable mark on Medieval history, documented across 87 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Shaped institutional landscape and community development. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The Last Supper profoundly influenced Western art and religious thought, shaping the portrayal of biblical narratives and human emotion in art, and inspiring countless artists and adaptations across centuries.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-28T20:57:45Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#30398235055", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "The Last Supper is a mural painting by the Italian High Renaissance artist Leonardo da Vinci, dated to c. 1495–1498, housed in the refectory of the Convent of Santa Maria delle Grazie in Milan, Italy. The painting represents the scene of the Last Supper of Jesus with the Twelve Apostles, as it is told in the Gospel of John – specifically the moment after Jesus announces that one of his apostles will betray him. Its handling of space, mastery of perspective, treatment of motion and complex display of human emotion has made it one of the Western world's most recognizable paintings and among Leonardo's most celebrated works.", "newValue": "The Last Supper, painted by Leonardo da Vinci between 1495 and 1498, is a seminal work of the Italian High Renaissance, located in the refectory of the Convent of Santa Maria delle Grazie in Milan, Italy. This mural captures the critical moment from the Gospel of John when Jesus reveals that one of his apostles will betray him, an event that has resonated through centuries of Christian thought and art. The composition, characterized by its innovative use of perspective, emotional depth, and intricate detail, marks a pivotal point in the evolution of Western art and religious representation.

The painting depicts the twelve apostles in dynamic responses to Jesus' announcement, showcasing da Vinci's mastery in portraying human emotion and interaction. The work was commissioned by Duke Ludovico Sforza of Milan as part of a renovation of the convent, symbolizing both the patronage of the arts during the Renaissance and the intertwining of religion and politics. As the painting began to deteriorate due to experimental techniques and environmental factors, it sparked discussions on preservation and restoration that continue to this day.

